cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use or discuss Jestyr in research, please cite it as below."
title: "Jestyr: a self-hosted systems language without lifetimes, held byte-identical across two compiler implementations"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Ezzat"
    given-names: "Adam"
version: 0.1.0-research
date-released: "2026-08-07"
license: "MIT OR Apache-2.0"
repository-code: "https://github.com/AdamEzzat1/Jestyr"
abstract: >-
  Jestyr is a from-scratch low-level systems language exploring how much
  memory safety a language can keep while dropping lifetimes entirely:
  second-class borrows with a structural escape checker, generation-checked
  references, and region arenas, validated by self-hosting the compiler in
  the language itself. The project maintains two independent compiler
  implementations (a Rust reference and the self-hosted port) whose C output
  is held byte-identical over a test corpus, a verified self-hosting fixed
  point, a gcc-only bootstrap seed, checked cost-model attributes, and a
  locked floating-point determinism contract.
keywords:
  - programming-languages
  - compilers
  - memory-safety
  - ownership
  - self-hosting
  - bootstrapping
  - determinism
